# sender.rules — yamlmuster ruleset for the campaign.yaml sender block.
# Wired in Wave 2c. Translated from validate_sender_block; loaded by
# spar::_yamlmuster_sender and driven by that proc.
#
# Data in = the whole campaign dict (cdata). Call shape: validate <cdata>
# -groups {sender}. The wrapper stamps contact_name "" and segment "" onto
# each issue via spar::_issue (matching the legacy issue shape), so the rules
# carry no consumer vocabulary.
#
# Legacy's early return (no sender block => skip smtp_* checks, 707-712) is
# reproduced STRUCTURALLY: with no `sender` key the traversal never
# descends to sender_block, so its three rules never run. No special rule.
#
# There is NO vocab rule here: legacy does not do a closed-vocabulary walk
# on the campaign or sender block. The -keys lists below exist only so the
# child edge resolves and for documentation; they are not enforced.

# ── Levels ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
level root         -keys {sender}
level sender_block -keys {smtp_host smtp_user smtp_port smtp_pass smtp_from}

child root sender dict sender_block

# ── Rules ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# sender_missing: legacy 707-712. `sender` block must be present.
rule require root sender -code sender_missing \
    -message {sender: block missing from campaign.yaml — required for sending.} \
    -groups {sender}

# smtp_host_missing: legacy 719-723. -nonblank fires on absent OR blank/
# whitespace, reproducing legacy's `string trim ... eq ""`.
rule require sender_block smtp_host -nonblank -code smtp_host_missing \
    -message {sender.smtp_host not set — required for sending.} \
    -groups {sender}

# smtp_user_missing: legacy 724-728.
rule require sender_block smtp_user -nonblank -code smtp_user_missing \
    -message {sender.smtp_user not set — required for sending; the keychain is keyed by (host, user).} \
    -groups {sender}

# smtp_port_non_numeric: legacy 729-733. Warning. %v = the port value.
# DOUBT: legacy guards with `smtp_port ne "" && !integer`, so a present-but-
# empty port does NOT fire; range -integer would fire on "" unless the
# engine skips empty/blank values. Range must skip an absent keypath (as in
# profile invalid_yield) AND a blank value to match legacy here. DRAFT-NOTES d.
rule range sender_block smtp_port -integer -severity warning \
    -code smtp_port_non_numeric \
    -message {sender.smtp_port '%v' is not numeric — expected an integer port (e.g. 587).} \
    -groups {sender}
